### Groups and Group Chat API Tests

This file exercises the group and group chat REST routes under
`/api/groups`. It creates a test group, adds and removes you as a member,
sends and reads chat messages, checks unread counts, and deletes the group
at the end. See docs/group-chat-architecture.md for how groups and chat fit
together, including the realtime WebSocket side, which a .http file cannot
test.

Install the **REST Client** extension in VS Code and select the `local` or
`deployed` environment. Configure the same `baseUrl`, `adminUid`, and
`adminPassword` variables used by `toolchain.http` in the Git-ignored
`.vscode/settings.json`. Never put passwords in this file.

Run the requests in order: later requests use the ids that requests 3 and 4
return. Chat history lives in S3, so on a server without AWS settings,
message reads return an empty list even after a send.

### 1. Sign in (every /api/groups route needs a signed-in account)

# @name loginGroups
POST {{baseUrl}}/authenticate
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json

{
  "uid": "{{adminUid}}",
  "password": "{{adminPassword}}"
}

### 2. Read every group -> 200 with a JSON array

GET {{baseUrl}}/api/groups
Accept: application/json

### 3. Create a test group -> 201 with the group
# It also creates the group's chat storage in S3 when S3 is configured.
# On a local SQLite server, the first group created after a restart can fail with
# 500 (SQLITE_BUSY_SNAPSHOT); send it again. See Known issues in the guide.

# @name createGroup
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/groups
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json

{
  "name": "REST Client Test Group",
  "period": "0",
  "course": "CSA",
  "memberIds": []
}

### 4. Read your own account, for its person id -> 200

# @name me
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/person/get
Accept: application/json

### 5. Read the test group by id -> 200

GET {{baseUrl}}/api/groups/{{createGroup.response.body.$.id}}
Accept: application/json

### 6. Search groups by name -> 200 with the matches

GET {{baseUrl}}/api/groups/search?name=REST%20Client%20Test
Accept: application/json

### 7. Add yourself to the test group -> 200

POST {{baseUrl}}/api/groups/{{createGroup.response.body.$.id}}/members/{{me.response.body.$.id}}
Accept: application/json

### 8. Read the groups you belong to -> 200, including the test group

GET {{baseUrl}}/api/groups/person/{{me.response.body.$.id}}
Accept: application/json

### 9. Send a chat message -> 200 with the group's messages
# The sender is the signed-in account; the request cannot choose it.

POST {{baseUrl}}/api/groups/chat/{{createGroup.response.body.$.id}}/messages
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json

{
  "message": "Hello from groups.http"
}

### 10. Read the chat messages -> 200 with an array (empty when S3 is not configured)

GET {{baseUrl}}/api/groups/chat/{{createGroup.response.body.$.id}}/messages
Accept: application/json

### 11. Read your unread counts across your groups -> 200

GET {{baseUrl}}/api/groups/chat/unread
Accept: application/json

### 12. Mark the test group read -> 200

POST {{baseUrl}}/api/groups/chat/{{createGroup.response.body.$.id}}/read

### 13. List the group's shared files -> 200 with an array

GET {{baseUrl}}/api/groups/chat/{{createGroup.response.body.$.id}}/files
Accept: application/json

### 14. Remove yourself from the test group -> 200

DELETE {{baseUrl}}/api/groups/{{createGroup.response.body.$.id}}/members/{{me.response.body.$.id}}
Accept: application/json

### 15. Delete the test group -> 200

DELETE {{baseUrl}}/api/groups/{{createGroup.response.body.$.id}}
Accept: application/json
